Lease-based models eliminate user capital requirements while maintaining manufacturer control of product lifecycles. Continuous material loops enabled by lease structures create economic incentives for product durability and refurbishment investment. Users gain flexibility to access latest products without ownership liabilities.

Lease models resolve conflict between manufacturer interests (frequent replacement) and environmental interests (extended lifecycles). Performance-based pricing creates incentives for products requiring minimal refurbishment rather than frequent replacement. Leasing creates valuable datasets regarding actual product durability and failure patterns informing future designs.